thor":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576890069,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODQ1NjI0MA=="},"target":"4ba85baf54c894c29c4432e7d288a396bfdb807239b3c74f48a07ddee33cd234","message":"\u003e nah, this is an intended behavior, i.e. \"by design\". We allow to `Dataset('path').create()` (in python analogue) a dataset which does not exist yet. But as @mih points above, this results in such new dataset not being added to current one as a subdataset (i.e. git submodule).\n\n@yarikoptic I am still confused why is `-d randomname` accepted by design. Normally the `-d` flag should point to the dataset to which an action is applied. If that dataset does not exist, why not considering that an error?","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576892348,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjkwMjQyNzcwNA=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576943700,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODE5MTA4OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3951#issuecomment-568191088"},"message":"Fyi, the patch worked and I managed to integrate the added file in the clone. One thing to note though, my initial `update --merge` tests worked relatively quickly, but when I switched to the real master dataset that contains all the file content, it took hours to finish the merge. I don't find a rationale for this. A merge should just integrate changes, not start to verify the annex content of all the present files. So, in my view, you integrate changes in a dummy clone with no file content, or integrate changes in the real clone that has the file contents should be the same. Any tip on what is happening in background not to be so?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576943700,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODU0NjQyMA=="},"target":"1467e943f3a998a2d5b2ad5a3dcbae6daac05a8dbaf0779e793a5112bcc45d88","message":"Fyi, the patch worked and I managed to integrate the added files in the clone. One thing to note though, my initial `update --merge` tests worked relatively quickly, but when I switched to the real master dataset that contains all the file content, it took hours to finish the merge. I don't find a rationale for this. A merge should just integrate changes, not start to verify the annex content of all the present files. So, in my view, you integrate changes in a dummy clone with no file content, or integrate changes in the real clone that has the file contents should be the same. Any tip on what is happening in background not to be so?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576959318,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODIwODQ1OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3951#issuecomment-568208459"},"message":"Yes, merges should not be too long... Please file a separate issue with more info. Use -l debug (there is also various environment variables to provide time stamps, check CONTRIBUTING.md) to see what steps actually take this time. Provide info about repo size (git annex info has some info as well)","files":null}]}